    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe new `deduplicateCustomerCode` method is a clean, additive, idiomatic addition:\n\n- **Right unit / placement**: It sits directly after `deriveCustomerCode` and is composed at the call site as `deduplicateCustomerCode(deriveCustomerCode(owner))`, keeping the \"derive then de-dup\" concerns cleanly separated rather than overloading `deriveCustomerCode`.\n- **Consistent with local idioms**: It follows the exact conventions of the surrounding private helpers \u2014 a focused private method, a Javadoc that even reuses the codebase's stock \"Evaluated before the owner is persisted, so it reflects the owners present at creation time\" phrasing, and the same `ownerRepository.findAll().stream()` access pattern used by `countNamesakes`, `findHouseholdMembers`, `countOwnersInCity`, etc.\n- **No duplication / no catch-all**: The logic isn't repeated elsewhere, and `ClinicServiceImpl` already hosts this exact family of \"derive/count/reject against existing owners\" helpers, so the new method is a natural member of that group rather than bloating an unrelated class.\n- **Reasonable signature**: Taking a `String baseCode` and returning a `String` (rather than mutating the `Owner`) keeps it pure and composable, matching the level of the other derivation helpers. Using `Collectors.toSet()` here (vs `.toList()` elsewhere) is appropriate given the repeated `contains` lookups.\n\nA maintainer of this codebase would find this natural. No structural changes needed.",
